{"id":"ae383d9d-ace1-460f-8354-be9703af3742","object":"chat.completion","created":1785037958,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    nodes = case['nodes']\\n    required_symbols = set(case['required_symbols'])\\n    # Build maps\\n    node_map = {n['id']: n for n in nodes}\\n    children_map = {n['id']: [] for n in nodes}\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        parent = n.get('parent')\\n        if parent is not None:\\n            children_map.setdefault(parent, []).append(n['id'])\\n    \\n    # Retained set initially all nodes\\n    retained = set(node_map.keys())\\n    removed = []\\n    \\n    # Sort nodes by depth descending, then id as tiebreaker\\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n['depth'], n['id']))\\n    \\n    for node in sorted_nodes:\\n        nid = node['id']\\n        if nid not in retained:\\n            continue  # already removed\\n        if not node['removable']:\\n            continue\\n        if node['symbol'] in required_symbols:\\n            continue\\n        if node['depth'] == 0:  # root\\n            continue\\n        # Check if all children are already removed\\n        children = children_map.get(nid, [])\\n        if any(child in retained for child in children):\\n            continue\\n        # Remove this node\\n        retained.remove(nid)\\n        removed.append(nid)\\n    \\n    retained_sorted = sorted(retained)\\n    return {\\\"removed_node_ids\\\": removed, \\\"retained_node_ids\\\": retained_sorted}\\n\"\n}","reasoning_content":"We need to implement `solve(case)` that takes a payload object with nodes and required_symbols. The task is SE-PE-01: grammar_guided_node_reduction. The description says: \"Reduce a registered parse tree through grammar-valid deletion and replacement candidates while preserving an interestingness oracle.\" Also: \"Consider deeper parse-tree nodes before shallower nodes. Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children. Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented. Use node ID as the stable tie break at equal depth. Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\"\n\nWe need to produce `removed_node_ids` and `retained_node_ids`. The output schema: array of strings for each. Required keys. Additional properties false.\n\nFrom the payload: nodes array with each node having id, parent, symbol, depth, removable, utility. Also required_symbols list. There is _case_nonce but ignore.\n\nThe algorithm: We have a tree (nodes with parent pointers). We need to \"reduce\" the tree by removing nodes that are removable, but must keep required symbols present. The goal: \"grammar-valid deletion and replacement candidates\" and \"preserving interestingness oracle\". Likely we need to remove nodes with highest utility? Or some pruning? The description says \"grammar_valid, interestingness_preserved, registered_candidate_order, stable_ties.\" There's no explicit grammar or interestingness function given. But we have `removable` flag and `utility`. Possibly we are to remove nodes that are removable, starting from deepest, to reduce tree while ensuring all required symbols remain. And we must not remove nodes that have children that are retained (i.e., we can only remove leaf nodes? Or nodes that are not needed? Need to interpret.\n\nConsider: \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" So we cannot remove a node if any of its children are retained. That implies we can only remove leaf nodes (nodes with no children that are retained). Also we cannot remove nodes whose symbol is in required_symbols. Also root (depth 0) cannot be removed. Also nodes with removable=false cannot be removed.\n\n\"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" That means after removal, the set of symbols in the remaining nodes must still include all required_symbols. So we check that the removed node's symbol is not the only occurrence of a required symbol? Actually \"all required symbols remain represented\" means that after removal, every required symbol must still be present in some node. So we cannot remove a node if its symbol is required and it's the only node with that symbol? But we already said we never remove a required symbol node. So that condition is automatically satisfied? Or maybe some nodes with non-required symbols might be the only providers of a required symbol? No, required symbols are attached to node symbols. If a node has a required symbol, we cannot remove it. So we only consider removable nodes with symbol not required. But after removal, we must ensure that all required symbols still exist. That's always true because we never remove required nodes. However, there could be a case where a required symbol exists in multiple nodes; we could remove some of them as long as at least one remains? But the rule says \"Never remove a required symbol\" – it doesn't say we can remove a node that has a required symbol if there is another node with same symbol. The rule explicitly says \"never remove ... a required symbol\". That suggests we cannot remove any node whose symbol is in required_symbols, regardless of duplicates. So we only consider nodes with symbol not in required_symbols.\n\n\"Consider deeper parse-tree nodes before shallower nodes.\" So we sort nodes by depth descending. At equal depth, use node ID as tie break (stable). Then for each node in that order, if it is removable (removable==true), its symbol not required, and it has no children that are retained (i.e., all children (if any) are removed already or never existed), and removing it does not cause any required symbol to become absent (which is automatically true since we don't remove required symbols), then we remove it. But note: children condition: we cannot remove a node if it has any children that are currently retained. So we need to process in a way that children are removed before parent. Since we process deepest first, that's already the case. However, we must also ensure that if a node has children that are not removable or not removed for other reasons, we cannot remove the parent. So we need to maintain a set of retained nodes. We start with all nodes retained. Then we iterate over nodes sorted descending depth (and tie ID). For each node, check conditions: removable, symbol not required, not root, and all children (if any) are not retained (i.e., either never existed or have already been removed). If conditions met, we remove it: add its id to removed_node_ids, remove from retained set. At the end, retained_node_ids is the remaining set sorted. Note that removal order is the order we removed nodes, which is by descending depth, tie ID. That matches \"actual removal order\". Output arrays sorted? The spec says \"Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\" So retained_node_ids should be sorted (presumably alphabetically or by ID? They are strings, so sort lexicographically). removed_node_ids should be in order of removal. But check example: In output schema, both are arrays of strings, no explicit sort requirement for removed, but text says \"actual removal order\". So we must output in the order we removed them. For retained, sort. That matches common practice.\n\nWhat about \"utility\"? Not used? Possibly it's for interestingness? But we don't have any interestingness oracle. The problem says \"preserving an interestingness oracle\" but no function given. Maybe utility is used as a threshold? Or we are to remove nodes with lowest utility? However the description of \"grammar-valid deletion and replacement candidates\" suggests we are to try to remove nodes that are removable and whose removal doesn't break grammar (i.e., the remaining tree is still a valid parse tree for some grammar?). That's vague. The fixtures show three cases with same structure: root, children n1 (sym s1, removable false), n2 (sym s2 removable true), n3 (sym s0 removable false). Then grandchildren: n4 (parent n2, sym s1 removable true), n5 (parent n3 sym s2 removable false), n6 (parent n1 sym s0 removable true). Required symbols: [\"root\",\"s1\"]. So we must keep root (depth0) and at least one node with symbol \"s1\". s1 appears in n1 (depth1, not removable) and n4 (depth2, removable). Since n1 is not removable, s1 is always retained via n1. So we can potentially remove n4? But n4's parent is n2, which is removable. Also n6 is removable with symbol s0 (not required). n5 is not removable. So we can try to remove nodes in depth order: deepest depth 2: n4, n5, n6. Among these, n5 is not removable, skip. n4 and n6 are removable and symbols not required. Need to check children: they are leaves (no children). So they can be removed. But before removing n4, we must check that removing it doesn't cause required symbols to be missing? s1 is already present in n1, so fine. So we can remove n4 and n6. Then depth 1: n1 (not removable), n2 (removable, symbol s2 not required), n3 (not removable). Check n2: its children? n4 has been removed, so n2 has no retained children. So we can remove n2. But note: if we remove n2, we must ensure required symbols still present. s1 still in n1, fine. Also root retained. So we can remove n2. Then root (depth0) cannot be removed. So removal order: n4, n6, n2? But depth order: n4, n5(not), n6, then n2. n4 and n6 have same depth 2. Tie break by ID: \"n4\" < \"n6\" lexicographically? Actually \"n4\" < \"n6\" because '4' < '6'? But IDs are strings: \"n4\" vs \"n6\". Typically lexicographic order: 'n' same, then '4' < '6', so n4 first, then n6. That matches fixture? Let's see if we can deduce expected output from given fixtures? The problem doesn't give expected outputs, only inputs. But we can reason that this is the likely algorithm. However, are we supposed to consider utility? The description says \"utility\" in node but no instruction. Maybe utility is used to decide which node to remove when multiple candidates? But tie-breaking by ID is specified. So utility might be irrelevant for this task. Possibly the \"interestingness oracle\" is based on utility threshold? No. The problem says \"preserving an interestingness oracle\" but never defines it. This might be a red herring or part of the metaphor. Maybe we need to ensure that after removal, the sum of utilities of remaining nodes is above some threshold? But no threshold given. Alternatively, maybe we are to remove nodes with lowest utility? But the algorithm description says \"Consider deeper parse-tree nodes before shallower nodes.\" and \"Use node ID as the stable tie break at equal depth.\" That suggests a deterministic order based on depth and ID, not utility. So we ignore utility.\n\nGiven the explicit constraints: \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" That matches our reasoning. Also \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" That is automatically satisfied because we never remove required symbol nodes.\n\nThus the algorithm is: Build a parent-child map. Initialize set of retained nodes = all node ids. Build a list of nodes sorted by (-depth, id). For each node in that order, if node.removable and node.symbol not in required_symbols and node.id != 'root' (root has depth 0 but also symbol 'root' often required, but we can check root by depth 0 or parent null) and all children (if any) are not in retained (i.e., are removed), then remove: add to removed list, remove from retained set. At end, retained list sorted ascending by id.\n\nCheck: If a node has children that were removed earlier (since children deeper processed first), then its children condition will be true. That's fine.\n\nEdge: what if a node has no children? Then condition vacuously true.\n\nAlso need to handle nodes with children that are not removable and thus never removed: then parent cannot be removed because children retained. That's correct.\n\nImplementation: parse case: case has nodes list and required_symbols list. Build dict: id->node. Also build dict: id->list of children ids. Then iterate sorted nodes. For each node, check: if not node['removable']: continue; if node['symbol'] in required_symbols: continue; if node['depth'] == 0: continue (root). Then check children: if any child_id in retained_set: continue; else remove.\n\nReturn {\"removed_node_ids\": removed_list (order of removal), \"retained_node_ids\": sorted(list(retained_set))}.\n\nBut should we also consider \"registered_candidate_order\"? That might be the order we consider candidates (depth then ID). That's what we do.\n\nNow output must be JSON object with these two arrays.\n\nThe overall implementation must be Python source that defines solve(case). The case argument is the payload object (dict). Must not mutate case.\n\nWrite code accordingly.\n\nPotential pitfalls: The nodes may not be sorted in input; we need to build mapping. Also ensure we use case as provided, not expect outer envelope. The fixture shows payload contains nodes and required_symbols. Also _case_nonce but ignore.\n\nWe'll implement solve(case) that returns dict.\n\nNow provide the JSON envelope with \"implementation\" key containing the source code as a JSON string, properly escaped.\n\nLet's write the code, then embed in JSON.\n\nWe'll also include docstring or comments? Not required but can be helpful.\n\nMake sure to use only Python standard library.\n\nNow produce final answer."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":2057,"completion_tokens":2982,"total_tokens":5039,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":2048},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":2619},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":2048,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":9},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20260402"}